WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.800
You now know the three parts of the cell theory.

2
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.900
But how did people come to know them?

3
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.060
Seeing cells was only the first step.

4
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.900
It started with a slice of cork.

5
00:00:15.883 --> 00:00:21.343
In 1665, Robert Hooke published a book of drawings made with his microscope.

6
00:00:22.643 --> 00:00:26.443
He cut a very thin slice of cork and shone light on it through a lens.

7
00:00:27.567 --> 00:00:31.567
He saw tiny spaces with thin walls between them, like a honeycomb.

8
00:00:32.927 --> 00:00:33.927
He called them cells.

9
00:00:35.227 --> 00:00:37.907
Under his lens, the boxes crossed the eye line.

10
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.840
Cork comes from the bark of a tree.

11
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:43.880
Look closely at the empty boxes.

12
00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.660
Were Hooke's boxes living cells, or something else?

13
00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:57.727
Something else. The cork cells were dead.

14
00:00:59.007 --> 00:01:03.647
Only their walls were left, so Hooke saw the outside of cells, not the living parts

15
00:01:03.647 --> 00:01:04.647
inside them.

16
00:01:06.317 --> 00:01:12.377
In a letter dated 1676, Antonie van Leeuwenhoek described drops of water.

17
00:01:13.697 --> 00:01:16.437
He used small microscopes with a single tiny lens.

18
00:01:17.837 --> 00:01:22.417
Water from his well looked clear, yet he reported many tiny living things in it.

19
00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:26.057
He checked one dish of rainwater again and again.

20
00:01:27.417 --> 00:01:29.497
At first he found nothing alive in it.

21
00:01:30.537 --> 00:01:34.077
Then one day he saw a few tiny creatures, and the next day more.

22
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.790
When a view was not clear, he said so and did not count it.

23
00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:44.410
His careful records showed that seeing nothing is not the same as nothing being

24
00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:45.410
there.

25
00:01:45.983 --> 00:01:47.923
Microscopes kept getting better.

26
00:01:49.203 --> 00:01:54.903
By about 1840, scientists had looked at many plants and animals, and found cells in

27
00:01:54.903 --> 00:01:58.983
them. That was strong evidence for the first part of the theory.

28
00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:01.907
But where do new cells come from?

29
00:02:03.087 --> 00:02:07.987
At first, some scientists thought new cells formed out of a clear fluid, a bit like

30
00:02:07.987 --> 00:02:12.667
growing crystals. Then a new cell would need no parent cell at all.

31
00:02:13.887 --> 00:02:16.387
That idea had to be tested by looking harder.

32
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:23.620
More observations, including work published in the 1850s, showed that new cells form

33
00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:24.620
when a cell divides.

34
00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.280
One cell becomes two.

35
00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.120
The theory dropped the wrong idea and kept the rest.

36
00:02:32.283 --> 00:02:34.803
The three parts did not arrive all at once.

37
00:02:35.963 --> 00:02:39.283
Many steps came after a better tool or a more careful look.

38
00:02:40.583 --> 00:02:43.763
Each one added evidence, and the cell theory grew from it.
